WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.640
Good morning, John. Last week, the consensus to my question about why

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.060
we like to dance to songs we don't understand the words to,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:06.900
was that we can hear the passion in the words

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:09.860
but then we can project our individual circumstances onto

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:13.280
their, like, blank canvas, which I thought was really quite nice, and made sense.

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:15.120
But then I got obsessed with what the words actually meant.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.920
So I recruited some translators through Twitter,

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:19.700
not to translate word for word, but to translate the meaning.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:22.480
And then, I crammed that meaning, or attempted to,

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.800
into like a cover of the song in English, but with the original meaning.

00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.720
So yeah, I'm probably about to ruin these songs for you by eliminating your ability

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.400
to project your subconscious onto the nonsense of their words.

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:34.000
I don't care, though. Deal.

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.400
[Gangnam Style - Psy]

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.700
Gangnam is a district in Seoul that is like, a super wealthy district,

00:01:09.700 --> 00:01:13.400
and a lot of people that don't live there try to act like they live there,

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.200
and that can result in people struggling terribly to maintain that image,

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.900
and we have this problem in America, too.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.800
Gangnam Style at first appears to be celebrating that culture,

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.440
but then it turns out in fact that it's mocking the culture of materialism and wealth.

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:26.400
So, that's pretty cool.

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:27.500
D-d-dance!
[Macarena - Los del Rio]

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.900
This was the only verse that was in the version of the Macarena that we heard,

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.700
and it sounds pretty innocuous, but then,

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.220
the next verse in the original version of the Macarena...

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:45.300
Macarena's boyfriend gets drafted into the army,

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:47.900
and then she "listens to her body" and has sex with two of his friends,

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:51.740
and then wants to go shopping a lot, and it turns out that she's just a terrible person.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.340
So, dance!
[Dragostea Din Tea - O Zone]

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.800
So this guy keeps calling this girl, who's presumably his ex or current girlfriend.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.600
She won't pick up, so he's leaving messages.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:28.900
He keeps talking about how cool he is,

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:32.600
and relating himself to all these epic Romanian cultural icons,

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.620
and then he's just wailing about how she's leaving him,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.380
and relating their love to a Romanian epic poem.

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:39.500
It's very sad. So dance party!
[Promise - Hirose Kohmi]

00:02:56.000 --> 00:02:56.700
I thought it was gonna be,

00:02:56.700 --> 00:02:59.260
"get down, I shake it and I shake it and I do a little dance."

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.780
Instead, she appears to be singing about the eternal pledge

00:03:01.780 --> 00:03:04.420
that she made to her dead or otherwise lost lover and

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:08.420
now is alone in the cold, cold winter, which, according to one of the translators

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:12.020
is a Japanese symbol for a perfect thing in an imperfect setting,

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.580
which I guess would be her perfect pledge to a dead guy.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:17.900
Get down!

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:19.100
I also translated Soulja Boy!
[Crank Dat - Soulja Boy]

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.740
Yeah. So. I never wanna dance to that song again.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.900
I apologize for ruining all these songs for you.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.660
Thank you to my Romanian and Korean and Japanese and Spanish translators,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:39.760
and thank you for Michael Aranda for mixing and auto-tuning those,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:42.260
'cause I can't do the things that those people do.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.640
And John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

